1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Okay, everyone, if you'd like to come over here to our next exhibit.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Okay, so we're continuing to learn about the history of the home computer.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Now, does anyone recognize this amazing machine?

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
No? Well, anyone who grew up in Britain in the 1980s would immediately recognize this.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
It's the famous BBC microcomputer. You've all heard of the BBC, right?

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Well, of course. It's the television company in England.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:32,000
The British Broadcasting Corporation, or BBC, is Britain's national, state-run television and radio network.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,000
So why did a TV station make a computer?

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
That's a really interesting story. It starts in the early 1980s.

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:46,000
BBC television showed a program called The Mighty Micro, in which a professor, his name was Dr. Christopher Evans,

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,000
he predicted the importance of the microcomputer, what we today just call the computer or the personal computer.

12
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Now, you have to remember that in 1980, there were no computers in homes, in schools, in libraries.

13
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Computers were big and expensive, so they still weren't something that a family or a student would go out and buy.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
But Dr. Evans made a prediction, a guess about the future.

15
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
He predicted that computers were going to get smaller.

16
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Technology was making it possible to build a whole computer in a box that could fit on your desk.

17
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
That's why the first home computers were called microcomputers.

18
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Micro meaning small.

19
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Okay, so, um, Evans argued that the microcomputer was going to change everything,

20
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,000
and that Britain's children needed to learn how to use a computer in order to be prepared for the future.

21
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Now, it just so happened that a lot of important people watched that program,

22
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
and Evans' predictions caught the attention of many people in Britain.

23
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
In fact, even the British Parliament, the government, ended up discussing the program and Evans' predictions.

24
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:57,000
So the BBC decided to start a new project to build a computer that was affordable, but that had lots of functions.

25
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Text, graphics, sound, music, programming, even artificial intelligence.

26
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
The BBC planned to sell its microcomputer to homes and especially schools around the country.

27
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
So the BBC made the actual computer?

28
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:14,000
No, actually they didn't.

29
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:22,000
The BBC was, and still is, in the business of making television and radio programs, not computers.

30
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
So they wanted to find a computer company that could make the computer and put the BBC's name on it.

31
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:35,000
In Cambridge, England, home to the famous old university, a small company called Acorn heard about the project.

32
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:42,000
It just so happened that Acorn was already making its first computer, but it wasn't quite finished yet.

33
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
The Acorn employees worked through the night and finished that first computer.

34
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,000
It was called the Proton.

35
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,000
They showed their brand new creation to the BBC and won the contract.

36
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:58,000
It's really an amazing story of a small company beating all the bigger corporations.

37
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,000
The first BBC computer, they called it the Model A, came out in 1981.

38
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,000
And the Model B came out not too long afterward.

39
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
And that's the computer you can see here.

40
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
The BBC Model B microcomputer.

41
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:21,000
The computer was an immediate success, mainly because schools in Britain bought many of them to teach computer skills to students.

42
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Excuse me, what could you do with a computer like this?

43
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
I mean, what was it capable of?

44
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Well, the B, that's the nickname for the BBC Model B microcomputer, was an amazing computer for its time.

45
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:39,000
You could write programs, draw pictures in eight colors, write music.

46
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
The computer could even speak words in English.

47
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
And you could play games.

48
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Today you would find them very simple, but they were pretty cool in the early 80s.

49
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,000
There was also a lot of educational software.

50
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Programs that taught children English, math, science, and history.

51
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Math teachers loved the BBC Buggy, a robot that you could control using a BBC computer.

52
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,000
And of course you could use the computer for word processing.

53
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,000
It was really new for most kids to type their essays and then print them.

54
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,000
It was very exciting.

55
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Was the BBC computer used in elementary schools?

56
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Eventually, yes, but not at first.

57
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:24,000
I have British friends who first used this computer when they were in middle or high school.

58
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Things have certainly changed, right?

59
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
I mean, nowadays some children are using computers even before they go to elementary school.

60
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Okay, any more questions before we move on to the next exhibit?

